///2INTRO
BRIEF INTRODUCTION by Kirk Lawrence

   The author's original documentation, which follows below, is somewhat
   convoluted and technical.  So here, in plain language, is what QSORT
   is all about:

   QSORT takes an ASCII text file, sorts each line of the file into "lexi-
   cographical" order, then writes the sorted file to disk.

   To QSORT, "lexicographical order" means "ASCII table order."  A line
   beginning with an ASCII 1 will be placed first in the sorted file; a
   line beginning with an ASCII 255 will be placed last in the sorted file.

   One result of this approach is that QSORT is case-sensitive...and lines
   beginning with capital letters will be sorted alphabetically and inserted
   into the sorted file AHEAD OF lines beginning with lower-case letters.

   Since QSORT begins sorting each line on the basis of the FIRST CHARACTER
   in the line, any leading "spaces" are interpreted as characters, and the
   line containing the leading "spaces" is placed into the sorted file on
   that basis.

   Therefore, to get an accurate sort, every line in the text file to be
   sorted should begin at the SAME left-margin column, with no varying
   indentations.

   The file to be sorted is loaded entirely into memory, so there must be
   sufficient computer memory available to accommodate the file, plus the
   loaded QSORT program.


///2USAGE
        Syntax: QSORT [d:]filename.ext  <enter>

   The drive letter [d:] is optional.  The input file "filename.ext" will be
   sorted, and written to disk as a file named "filename.SRT."  The .SRT ex-
   tension of the sorted file is hard-wired into the program, and cannot be
   changed unless you change and re-compile the source code.  The original
   file remains intact, and is not altered in any way.

-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-



///2TECHNICAL
Following is the author's original documentation:
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Sorting is a frequent application of computer usage, particularly when
large amounts of data are involved. Many schemes exist to accomplish a
sort, so many that their discussion occupies the whole third volume of
Knuth's series on The Art of Computer Programming.

One of the more popular schemes is called Quicksort; it was introduced
in 1962 by C.A.R. Hoare. The name is descriptive, since it tends to
complete a sort in a time proportional to N*log N, rather than time N**2
which is characteristic of more straightforward procedures.

This does not imply that Quicksort is not also straightforward, but it
required some careful thought to figure it out and establish its speed
at a time when such things were not always given much consideration.

The basic idea is simple - two pointers are started out at the ends of
the list that is going to be sorted, and all comparisons are made to
the first element of the list, which can be set aside during the sort.

The end pointer is moved backward until a smaller element is found than
the first one. It is put in first place and the beginning pointer moved
forward until a larger element is found, to be put up with the end pointer.

The pointers are alternately moved, bringing small elements to the front
of the list and large elements toward the end of the list. Eventually
the pointers meet, at which point the original element is deposited. Of
course, the list is probably not in order, but at least it has been split
into two parts.

The next step depends upon realizing that these two parts can be sorted
independently, because every element in the lower portion is less than
every element in the upper portion.
Here is an ideal recursive process, because the separate portions can
each be attacked by the same process, which terminates when the segment
involved consists of only one element which means that the two pointers
already coincide.

The evaluation of the process is somewhat statistical, because the list
is not always split evenly - this depends on whether the first element
lies in the middle of the range or not. In fact if the list is already
in order, only the first element will be split out each time, and the
full N*(N-1)/2 steps will be required. However if the list begins in a
state of disorder, the question is how many times it has to be split in
half, which is log-base-2 of N. But we still have to run through the list
so the number of steps averages out N*log N.

QSORT is a Quicksort designed to place the lines of a text file in order.
It begins by reading the whole file into memory, whose size is therefore
a limit on the size of the file that can be sorted. This is not much of a
limitation, because larger files could be broken up, sorted individually,
and merged. However QSORT does not take this into account.

Since it is too much trouble to move lines around as required by Quicksort,
an index to the lines is constructed, running back from high memory. It is
composed of two-byte pointers to the actual lines, and it is the pointers
which are sorted rather than the lines themselves.

Although Knuth has some hesitation about the recursive aspects of Quicksort,
it should be noted that he wrote that particular volume before the prevalence
of computer architectures which facilitate recursion. Nevertheless, it is
necessary to reserve a rather larger stack than is customary because of the
number of intermediate pointers that may accumulate.

When the sorting has been finished, the sorted file is written on disk by
writing the lines into the new file in the order determined by the sorted
pointers. Since it is likely that the original file should be preserved,
a new file with the extension .SRT is created. QSORT should therefore be
used on a disk which has ample space for a new copy of the sorted file.

QSORT was written with a specific purpose in mind. Therefore, it does not
break up files too large for memory capacity. Nor does it allow the sorted
file to be placed on another disk. Full lines are ordered lexicographically
in ASCII order. ASCII order places control characters such as tabs and the
carriage return before printable characters; it also places numbers and
most punctuation before letters, but it places uppercase before lower case.

There will most likely be applications in which lines would be sorted by
partial fields, not necessarily in the order of their appearance on the
line. The subroutine which evaluates the relative ordering of two lines
would have to be modified to accomodate a different sorting criterion.
